{"meta":{"chapter":"03","section":"02","title":"Method of Joints","course":"statics","learning_objectives":[{"id":"statics-03-02-lo-01","statement":"Apply the method of joints to analyze trusses"},{"id":"statics-03-02-lo-02","statement":"Draw FBDs for individual joints"},{"id":"statics-03-02-lo-03","statement":"Solve for member forces systematically"},{"id":"statics-03-02-lo-04","statement":"Determine if members are in tension or compression"}],"concept_ids":["method_of_joints","joint_equilibrium","member_force"],"prerequisites":["truss","two_force_member"],"prereq_section_ids":["statics/03/01"],"practice_ids":["statics_joints_starting_joint","statics_joint_sign_interpretation","statics_joint_equations","statics_member_force_reasoning"],"estimated_time_minutes":55,"lo_practice_map":{"statics-03-02-lo-01":["statics_joint_equations","statics_member_force_reasoning"],"statics-03-02-lo-02":["statics_joint_equations"],"statics-03-02-lo-03":["statics_joints_starting_joint"],"statics-03-02-lo-04":["statics_joint_sign_interpretation"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Method of Joints\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Concept relationship map connecting Method Of Joints, Joint Equilibrium, and Member Force within Method of Joints.\" conceptid=\"method_of_joints\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/reference-figures/statics/03-02-method-of-joints.png\" alt=\"Method of Joints concept map with Method Of Joints, Joint Equilibrium, and Member Force connected to the section focus.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n> **Analyze one joint at a time, and the member-force pattern begins to reveal itself.**\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Roof That Reads Like a Spreadsheet\n\nWalk into any warehouse, gymnasium, or train shed and look up. The triangulated steel overhead is a truss, and every diagonal you see is carrying a specific number of kilonewtons in either tension or compression. A roof truss in a snow-loaded region might collapse not because a member \"broke\" abstractly, but because one slender diagonal that the engineer expected to pull in tension actually pushed in compression and buckled. The difference between a tie that can be a thin rod and a strut that must be a stocky tube comes down to a single algebraic sign.\n\nThe method of joints is how that sign gets pinned down. It turns a whole structure into a sequence of tiny, identical equilibrium problems, one joint at a time, each one no harder than balancing forces on a particle. By the end you know not just whether the structure stands, but exactly how hard each member is working and in which sense.\n\n> **The Big Picture:** Once you can read a truss joint by joint, you can size members, flag buckling-prone struts, and spot members that carry almost nothing. The same two scalar equations that balanced a hanging lamp now map the entire load path of a bridge or roof.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you should be able to:\n\n1. **Apply** the method of joints to a planar truss after support reactions are known.\n2. **Draw** a correct free-body diagram for an individual joint.\n3. **Solve** member forces using the two joint-equilibrium equations.\n4. **Interpret** the algebraic sign of each result as tension or compression.\n5. **Recognize** zero-force members and use them to shorten the analysis.\n\n---\n\n## Why the Method Works\n\nA truss is built from **two-force members**: straight bars pin-connected at their ends, loaded only at the joints. Because a two-force member carries force only along its own axis, the only unknown for each member is a single scalar magnitude (plus a sign telling us tension or compression).\n\nNow isolate a single joint. All the member forces meeting there are concurrent: they pass through one point. A point cannot rotate under concurrent forces, so the moment equation is automatically satisfied and gives nothing new. That leaves exactly two useful equations per joint in 2D:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0, \\qquad \\sum F_y = 0\n$$\n\nTwo equations means we can solve at most **two unknown member forces** at any single joint. That single fact drives the entire strategy: always begin at a joint where no more than two members are still unknown, solve them, and let those now-known forces unlock the next joint.\n\n> **Key Insight:** The method is not random joint-hopping. It is a deliberate sequence chosen so that every joint you visit has exactly two (or fewer) unknowns when you arrive.\n\nAt a support joint A carrying an upward reaction $A_y$, with one inclined member rising at angle $\\theta$ and one horizontal member, vertical equilibrium $A_y + F_{AC}\\sin\\theta = 0$ fixes the inclined force, and horizontal equilibrium $F_{AB} + F_{AC}\\cos\\theta = 0$ then fixes the chord. Explore how the reaction and angle drive both:\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Joint A equilibrium: reaction and incline angle drive the member forces\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"Ay\",\"label\":\"Vertical reaction Ay\",\"min\":1,\"max\":20,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"kN\",\"default\":5},{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Incline angle theta\",\"min\":15,\"max\":75,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":30}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Inclined member F_AC (+T / -C)\",\"unit\":\"kN\",\"expr\":\"-Ay / sin(theta*3.14159265/180)\"},{\"label\":\"Bottom chord F_AB (+T / -C)\",\"unit\":\"kN\",\"expr\":\"Ay * cos(theta*3.14159265/180) / sin(theta*3.14159265/180)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nNotice that a shallower incline (small $\\theta$) makes both members work much harder: as $\\sin\\theta$ shrinks, the compression in $AC$ blows up, which is exactly why very flat roof trusses need heavy chords.\n\n---\n\n## Tension-First Sign Convention\n\nBefore you know an answer, you do not know whether a member pushes or pulls. The discipline that removes the guesswork is a fixed convention:\n\n- **Draw every unknown member force as tension**, an arrow pointing *away from the joint*, along the member.\n- Solve the equilibrium equations as written.\n- **Read the sign of the result.**\n\n| Result | Meaning | Physical sense |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Positive ($+$) | Tension, as assumed | Member pulls on the joint; it is being stretched |\n| Negative ($-$) | Compression | Member pushes on the joint; it is being squeezed |\n\nA negative number is not a mistake and does not mean you must redo the joint with a flipped arrow. The magnitude is correct; the sign simply reports the true sense. Because every member is assumed tensile everywhere, the same member force keeps one consistent symbol as you carry it from joint to joint.\n\n> **Critical Check:** When member AC is shown as tension at joint A (arrow away from A toward C), Newton's third law requires the *same* tension to appear at joint C as an arrow pointing away from C toward A. The two arrows point in opposite global directions but represent one identical scalar $F_{AC}$.\n\n---\n\n## Standard Procedure\n\nUse this workflow every time:\n\n1. **Solve the support reactions** from whole-structure equilibrium ($\\sum F_x = 0$, $\\sum F_y = 0$, $\\sum M = 0$).\n2. **Pick a starting joint** with no more than two unknown member forces (a support joint is usually ideal).\n3. **Draw the joint free-body diagram**, showing external loads, support reactions, and each unknown member force as tension.\n4. **Resolve** each inclined member force into $x$ and $y$ components.\n5. **Apply** $\\sum F_x = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = 0$ and solve the two unknowns.\n6. **Interpret** the signs (positive = tension, negative = compression).\n7. **Advance** to an adjacent joint, now using the member forces you just found.\n\nThe procedure is repetitive by design. Reliability comes from disciplined sequencing, not cleverness.\n\nPut the workflow in the correct order to internalize the sequencing discipline:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Solve the support reactions from whole-structure equilibrium\",\"Pick a starting joint with no more than two unknown member forces\",\"Draw the joint FBD with every unknown member as tension\",\"Resolve each inclined member force into x and y components\",\"Apply the two scalar equilibrium equations and solve\",\"Interpret each sign: positive is tension, negative is compression\",\"Advance to an adjacent joint using the forces just found\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n<inline-check question=\"You arrive at a joint that still has three unknown member forces. What should you do?\" explanation=\"A planar joint provides only two scalar equations, so it can resolve at most two unknowns. Solve a different, lower-unknown joint first to make one of these three forces known before returning here.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Solve a different joint first to reduce this one to two unknowns\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Use the moment equation at the joint for a third equation\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Assume one member is zero-force so only two remain\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 1: Symmetric Triangular Truss\n\n**Problem.** A simple triangular truss has a pin at A$(0,0)$, a roller at B$(6\\text{ m}, 0)$, and an apex at C. The two inclined members AC and BC each rise at $30^\\circ$ above the horizontal, so the apex height is $h = 3\\tan 30^\\circ = 1.732$ m. A vertical load of $10$ kN acts downward at C. Find the force in every member and label it tension or compression.\n\n**Step 1: Support reactions.**\n\nBy symmetry of geometry and loading, each vertical support carries half the load:\n\n$$\nA_y = B_y = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ kN (up)}, \\qquad A_x = 0\n$$\n\n(The pin's horizontal reaction is zero because no horizontal load is applied.)\n\n**Step 2: Start at joint A** (a support joint with only two unknowns, $F_{AB}$ and $F_{AC}$). Draw the FBD: reaction $A_y = 5$ kN up, member AB pulling horizontally toward B ($+x$), member AC pulling up the incline at $30^\\circ$. Both members assumed in tension.\n\nVertical equilibrium first, because only $F_{AC}$ has a vertical component:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad A_y + F_{AC}\\sin 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n5 + F_{AC}(0.5) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AC} = -10 \\text{ kN}\n$$\n\nThe negative sign means AC is in **compression**:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{F_{AC} = 10 \\text{ kN (C)}}\n$$\n\nNow horizontal equilibrium:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad F_{AB} + F_{AC}\\cos 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nF_{AB} + (-10)(0.866) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AB} = 8.66 \\text{ kN}\n$$\n\nPositive, so AB is in **tension**:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{F_{AB} = 8.66 \\text{ kN (T)}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Joint B by symmetry.** The mirror-image joint gives\n\n$$\n\\boxed{F_{BC} = 10 \\text{ kN (C)}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Verify at joint C.** The apex sees the $10$ kN load down plus both inclined members. With AC and BC each in $10$ kN compression (pushing *toward* the joint, i.e. up the inclines from C's view), the vertical check is\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = -10 + 10\\sin 30^\\circ + 10\\sin 30^\\circ = -10 + 5 + 5 = 0 \\;\\checkmark\n$$\n\nand the horizontal components ($10\\cos 30^\\circ$ left and right) cancel. The solution closes. The result also makes engineering sense: the inclined top members are squeezed (the load pushes the apex down onto them) while the bottom chord is stretched as it ties the two feet together.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Asymmetric Loading (Full Workflow)\n\n**Problem.** Same triangular geometry (A pin at origin, B roller at $6$ m, apex C at height $h = 1.732$ m), but now joint C carries a downward load of $10$ kN *and* a horizontal load of $4$ kN to the right. Find all member forces.\n\n**Step 1: Support reactions (the symmetry shortcut is gone, so use full equilibrium).**\n\nHorizontal: the pin must absorb the horizontal load.\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad A_x + 4 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_x = -4 \\text{ kN} \\;(\\text{i.e. } 4 \\text{ kN to the left})\n$$\n\nMoment about A (counterclockwise positive). The roller reaction $B_y$ acts at $x = 6$; the $10$ kN down acts at $x = 3$; the $4$ kN horizontal acts at height $h = 1.732$:\n\n$$\n\\sum M_A = 0: \\quad B_y(6) - 10(3) - 4(1.732) = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nB_y = \\frac{30 + 6.928}{6} = 6.155 \\text{ kN (up)}\n$$\n\nVertical:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad A_y + B_y - 10 = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; A_y = 10 - 6.155 = 3.845 \\text{ kN (up)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Joint A** (knowns $A_x = -4$, $A_y = 3.845$; unknowns $F_{AB}$ along $+x$, $F_{AC}$ up the $30^\\circ$ incline, both tension-assumed).\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad A_y + F_{AC}\\sin 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n3.845 + F_{AC}(0.5) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\boxed{F_{AC} = -7.69 \\text{ kN} = 7.69 \\text{ kN (C)}}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = 0: \\quad A_x + F_{AB} + F_{AC}\\cos 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n-4 + F_{AB} + (-7.69)(0.866) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; F_{AB} = 4 + 6.66 = \\boxed{10.66 \\text{ kN (T)}}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Joint B** (roller reaction $B_y = 6.155$ up; member BA now known, pulling toward A in the $-x$ direction at $10.66$ kN tension; unknown $F_{BC}$ up the incline toward C). The unit vector from B to C is $(-\\cos 30^\\circ, \\sin 30^\\circ)$.\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad B_y + F_{BC}\\sin 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n6.155 + F_{BC}(0.5) = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\boxed{F_{BC} = -12.31 \\text{ kN} = 12.31 \\text{ kN (C)}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Horizontal check at joint B.** Member BA pulls in $-x$ ($-10.66$ kN); member BC contributes $F_{BC}\\cos 30^\\circ$ in the $-x$ sense, which is $(-12.31)(-0.866) = +10.66$ kN:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = -10.66 + 10.66 = 0 \\;\\checkmark\n$$\n\nThe check closes, confirming the reactions and member forces are mutually consistent. Notice how the $4$ kN horizontal push made the two inclined members carry *different* compressions ($7.69$ vs $12.31$ kN): the windward and leeward sides of a loaded truss rarely work equally hard, which is exactly the kind of result that drives unequal member sizing in real design.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Track the asymmetric-load solution and predict each result\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"A 4 kN horizontal load acts to the right at apex C, plus the 10 kN down. From sum Fx = 0 on the whole truss, what is the pin horizontal reaction Ax?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Ax = -4 kN (4 kN to the left)\",\"Ax = +4 kN (4 kN to the right)\",\"Ax = 0, the roller takes it\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"The roller carries no horizontal force, so the pin must absorb all of it: Ax + 4 = 0 gives Ax = -4 kN, i.e. 4 kN to the left.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Taking moments about A, By(6) - 10(3) - 4(1.732) = 0. What is By?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"By = 6.155 kN up\",\"By = 5.0 kN up\",\"By = 3.845 kN up\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"By = (30 + 6.928)/6 = 6.155 kN up. The horizontal load adds a tipping moment, so By is no longer the symmetric 5 kN.\"},{\"prompt\":\"At joint B, vertical equilibrium is 6.155 + F_BC sin(30) = 0. What is F_BC?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"F_BC = -12.31 kN, so 12.31 kN compression\",\"F_BC = -7.69 kN, so 7.69 kN compression\",\"F_BC = +12.31 kN, so 12.31 kN tension\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"F_BC = -6.155/0.5 = -12.31 kN. The leeward incline (BC) carries far more compression than the windward one (AC, 7.69 kN): asymmetric loading splits the work unequally.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n---\n\n## Zero-Force Members\n\nSome members carry no load under a given loading. Spotting them before you compute saves time and clarifies the load path. Two rules cover most cases:\n\n1. **Two-member joint, no external load, members not collinear:** both members are zero-force. With only two non-aligned members and nothing else at the joint, each component equation forces each member to zero.\n2. **Three-member joint, two members collinear, no external load:** the third (non-collinear) member is zero-force, and the two collinear members carry equal force.\n\n**Quick example.** Consider a joint J where a horizontal bottom chord runs straight through (members JL and JR collinear), a vertical member JV rises, and no external load or support acts at J. Taking $y$ perpendicular to the collinear chord:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0: \\quad F_{JV} = 0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\boxed{F_{JV} = 0}\n$$\n\nThe vertical member carries nothing for this loading. It is not useless: it braces the long chord against buckling and will carry force under different loads. But for the present analysis it can be set aside, and the two chord segments simply transmit equal force through the joint.\n\n> **Why this matters:** Identifying zero-force members first can collapse a frightening-looking truss into just a handful of joints you actually need to solve.\n\n---\n\n## Efficiency Tips\n\nThe method becomes much faster if you scan for these before grinding through equations:\n\n- **Symmetry** in geometry *and* loading (solve half, mirror the rest).\n- **Zero-force members** (eliminate them up front).\n- **Joints already rich with known forces** (fewer unknowns to chase).\n- **Repeated member angles** that reuse the same sine and cosine values.\n\nAlways choose the next joint strategically. A joint with three unknowns is simply not ready, even if it sits right next to you.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Method of Joints\" context=\"A structural intern must determine whether the diagonal in a lightweight roof truss is in tension or compression before choosing between a cable-like member and a buckling-sensitive compression member.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Common Errors\n\nMost mistakes trace back to one of these:\n\n- starting at a joint with more than two unknowns,\n- forgetting to include support reactions as external joint forces,\n- mixing up or mis-measuring member angles,\n- misreading a negative sign (it means compression, not \"wrong math\"),\n- losing the consistent tension assumption when moving between joints.\n\nA useful self-check at every joint:\n\n- Do I have at most two unknowns here?\n- Are my components aligned with the correct axes?\n- Does the final sign make physical sense given how the load pushes on this region?\n\n---\n\n## Where the Method Becomes Less Efficient\n\nThe method of joints is reliable, but it is not always the fastest tool. It loses its edge when:\n\n- the target member lies deep inside a large truss (you must crawl through many joints to reach it),\n- several joints near the support already contain many unknowns,\n- or the design question concerns only one or two critical members.\n\nIn those situations the **method of sections** can reach a single interior member force in one cut and three equations, without solving every intermediate joint. The two methods are complementary: joints for a full member-by-member map, sections for a fast answer at one location.\n\nThis is also where engineering judgment begins. A member found in compression is not just a \"negative force.\" It may become a buckling problem, a slenderness problem, or a connection-stiffness problem. The analytical sign is the start of design thinking, not the end of it. When one diagonal consistently appears in compression while another stays in tension, member selection becomes disciplined: slender ties, stocky struts, and matched connection details follow the actual force path instead of rough intuition.\n\n---\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\n| Application | How the method of joints is used |\n| --- | --- |\n| Roof and floor trusses | Map every chord and web force to size ties vs buckling-prone struts |\n| Pratt / Howe / Warren bridges | Identify which diagonals carry tension vs compression across the span |\n| Transmission and antenna towers | Resolve wind-induced member forces joint by joint in a space-truss simplification |\n| Crane booms and lifting frames | Confirm tension-only members never see reversal under different boom angles |\n| Stadium and exhibition canopies | Locate zero-force and lightly loaded members for weight optimization |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** The same two equations, $\\sum F_x = 0$ and $\\sum F_y = 0$, that solved a hanging lamp now resolve every joint of a 100-meter roof truss. Only the bookkeeping grows; the physics is identical.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Joint equilibrium (2D) | $\\sum F_x = 0, \\;\\; \\sum F_y = 0$ |\n| Inclined member components | $F_x = F\\cos\\theta, \\;\\; F_y = F\\sin\\theta$ |\n| Reaction from moment balance | $\\sum M_A = 0$ to isolate one support |\n| Sign convention | $+ \\Rightarrow$ tension, $\\; - \\Rightarrow$ compression |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Treat each joint as a particle: two scalar equations, at most two unknown members.\n- Solve support reactions first; they are known external forces at the early joints.\n- Assume every unknown member is in tension, then let the sign report the truth.\n- A negative answer means compression, with the magnitude already correct.\n- Zero-force members and symmetry can dramatically shorten the work.\n- Carry each member force with a consistent symbol from joint to joint.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Section 3.3: Method of Sections**, we cut straight through a truss to reach an interior member force in a single step, the fast counterpart to the joint-by-joint sweep you just mastered. The two methods together give you both a complete force map and a quick targeted answer for any planar truss.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why is it standard practice to begin the method of joints at a joint with no more than two unknown member forces?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because each joint in a planar truss gives only two equilibrium equations.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because truss joints can never have more than two connected members.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because support reactions are never needed for truss analysis.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. A planar joint gives two scalar equilibrium equations, so more than two unknown member forces cannot be solved directly at that stage.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"method_of_joints\",\"joint_equilibrium\",\"member_force\"]'></concept-map>\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"statics_joints_starting_joint","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"method_of_joints","concept_ids":["method_of_joints","joint_equilibrium"],"stem":"Which joint is the best starting point for the method of joints?","options":["A joint with four unknown member forces","A joint with two or fewer unknown member forces","Any joint chosen at random","Only the center joint of the truss"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Each joint in a planar truss gives two equilibrium equations, so you want no more than two unknown member forces.","misconception_id":"start_any_joint","learning_objective_ids":["statics-03-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"statics_joint_sign_interpretation","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"joint_equilibrium","concept_ids":["joint_equilibrium","member_force"],"stem":"A member force was assumed to be in tension, but the solved value is negative. What does that mean?","options":["The member force is zero","The truss is unstable","The member is actually in compression","The equations of equilibrium do not apply"],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"With the standard tension-first convention, a negative result means the actual force direction is opposite to the assumed tensile direction, so the member is in compression.","misconception_id":"negative_force_means_wrong_math","learning_objective_ids":["statics-03-02-lo-04"]},{"id":"statics_joint_equations","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"joint_equilibrium","concept_ids":["joint_equilibrium","method_of_joints"],"stem":"Which equilibrium equations are applied directly at an ideal truss joint in 2D?","options":["sum M = 0 only","sum Fx = 0 and sum Fy = 0","sum Fx = 0, sum Fy = 0, and sum M = 0 for the joint as a rigid body","Bernoulli's equation"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An ideal joint is treated as a particle, so the direct equations are sum Fx = 0 and sum Fy = 0.","learning_objective_ids":["statics-03-02-lo-01","statics-03-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"statics_member_force_reasoning","type":"multiple_choice","difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"member_force","concept_ids":["member_force","method_of_joints"],"stem":"Why must support reactions usually be found before joint-by-joint analysis?","options":["Because member forces cannot exist without support reactions being exactly zero","Because the support reactions are external known loads needed in the first joint free-body diagrams","Because the method of joints replaces all supports with rollers","Because support reactions are used only for drawing diagrams, not equations"],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Support reactions act as known external forces on the first solvable joints, so they must usually be determined first.","misconception_id":"skip_support_reactions","learning_objective_ids":["statics-03-02-lo-01"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"start_any_joint","pattern":"solution_strategy_error","description":"Thinking the method of joints can begin at any joint regardless of unknown count","trigger":"learner starts at a joint with three or more unknown member forces","feedback":"In planar trusses, each joint gives only two equilibrium equations, so you should begin with a joint having no more than two unknown member forces.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"negative_force_means_wrong_math","pattern":"sign_interpretation_error","description":"Treating a negative member force as a mistake instead of a compression result","trigger":"learner discards a negative answer as invalid","feedback":"If you assumed tension first, a negative value usually means the member is actually in compression.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"skip_support_reactions","pattern":"setup_error","description":"Trying to solve joints before determining support reactions","trigger":"learner omits support reactions from the starting joint analysis","feedback":"Support reactions are external forces on the truss and must usually be found first so the initial joint equations are solvable.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"85903c36eb97dd07e5b339e055fbdeeaaecc467f33b2e78e824d374dd82c3cc6","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}